## Why This Question Is Asked
This question evaluates practical knowledge of serverless data pipelines on GCP, including error handling, data enrichment, and idempotent design patterns.

## Key Concepts
- Cloud Storage triggers and CSV validation
- Header normalization and PII masking
- Pub/Sub as a decoupling layer between ingestion and processing
- Dataflow streaming for real-time enrichment and aggregation
- BigQuery insertId for deduplication and idempotency
- Least-privilege IAM bindings
- Exponential backoff retry strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
// Cloud Function: triggered on GCS object finalization
exports.processTelemetry = (event, context) => {
  const file = event.bucket + '/' + event.name;

  return validateAndPublish(file)
    .then(() => console.log(`Processed: ${file}`))
    .catch(err => {
      console.error(`Error processing ${file}:`, err);
      throw err; // Triggers retry with exponential backoff
    });
};

async function validateAndPublish(file) {
  const [content] = await bucket.file(event.name).download();
  const rows = parseCSV(content);
  const validRows = rows.filter(validateRowSchema);
  const normalizedRows = validRows.map(normalizeHeaders).map(maskPII);

  await publisher.publish(normalizedRows);
}
```

## Evaluation Criteria
- Correct trigger configuration and bucket path pattern
- Schema validation logic implementation
- PII masking approach (field-level redaction)
- Idempotent BigQuery writes via insertId
- Appropriate retry/backoff configuration

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design an end-to-end data governance workflow with real-time drift detection across tenants, leveraging Data Catalog tagging, BigQuery taxonomy, and alerting. Focuses on correctness, security, and scalability trade-offs.

## Key Concepts

- Data taxonomy in BigQuery and how to surface it for tagging
- Data Catalog tagging API for dynamic resource labeling
- Drift detection patterns in streaming pipelines
- Idempotent processing guarantees in Dataflow and BigQuery inserts
- Observability and alert routing to Cloud Run/Slack and BigQuery summaries

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: apply a Data Catalog tag to a resource
const {DataCatalogClient} = require('@google-cloud/datacatalog');
const client = new DataCatalogClient();
async function tagResource(resourceName, tagTemplate, fields) {
  const [tag] = await client.createTag({
    parent: resourceName,
    tag: {
      template: tagTemplate,
      fields: fields
    }
  });
  return tag;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle taxonomy evolution without breaking existing tags?
- What metrics would you monitor to balance false positives vs drift detection latency?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a real-time data pipeline with end-to-end correctness, cross-project IAM, and robust operability under outages.

## Key Concepts
- Pub/Sub ingestion and dead-letter handling
- Dataflow (Apache Beam) streaming with exactly-once semantics
- BigQuery streaming inserts with insertId for idempotency
- Cross-project IAM and least privilege
- Canary rollout and observability (dashboards, lag alerts)

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: generate insertId function for BigQuery streaming inserts
function buildRecord(event) {
  return {
    insertId: `${event.deviceId}|${event.messageId}|${event.timestamp}`,
    json: {
      deviceId: event.deviceId,
      region: event.region,
      timestamp: event.timestamp,
      metrics: event.metrics
    }
  };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes ability to design scalable, secure, multi-tenant data pipelines on GCP, balancing isolation, cost control, and reliability in real-world enterprises.

## Key Concepts
- Pub/Sub and Dataflow streaming, exactly-once or dedup strategies
- BigQuery partitioning, per-tenant isolation with Authorized Views and row-level security
- CMEK for Cloud Storage staging, IAM least privilege
- Audit logging, monitoring, cost controls, testing strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
// Deduplication helper (conceptual)
function isDuplicate(event, seen) {
  const key = event.event_id + '|' + event.event_time;
  if (seen.has(key)) return true;
  seen.add(key);
  return false;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate idempotency in production?
- What rollback strategy for failed batches might you implement?
